Break out your textbooks, friends.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.640
We're jumping back into some high school biology

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.000
and hereditary genetics.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.440
When it comes to physical characteristics

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.200
like eye color, blood type, whatever,

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.600
it all comes down to dominant and recessive genes.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.760
You've probably heard this like a thousand times before,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.200
so we won't dwell on it too long.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.960
Basically, your parents each have two genes,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.240
and both parents pass down

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.920
just one of those genes to their children.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.240
Some of these genes take priority over others,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.240
thus making them the dominant gene.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.400
So let's say your parents pass down a type A

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.160
and a type O blood type gene.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.760
You'll end up with type A blood

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.120
because it's the dominant gene there.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.560
But if both your parents pass down

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.240
the recessive type O gene,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.560
even if both your parents have type A blood themselves,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.960
then you're gonna still end up with type O blood.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.960
You can actually map out all these potential outcomes

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:49.840
and how likely they are

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.520
with a helpful little diagram called

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.400
a Punnett Square.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.160
Which again, we've talked about plenty in the past,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.040
let's not dwell.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.560
But all of that being said,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.400
that whole dominant recessive gene thing,

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.760
it is one of those big lies

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.280
that tend to get taught in schools

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.880
because genetics, go figure,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.080
is a lot more complicated

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.560
than the shorthand given to it in those textbooks.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.000
Case in point, hair color.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.120
Instead of being a simple case of

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.360
the brunette gene is dominant over the blonde gene,

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.960
there's way more going on

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.360
in a system called incomplete dominance.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.680
Imagine you have a red flower and a white flower.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.200
In a typical Punnett Square system,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.200
you might think that the red would be the dominant color,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.480
so if you breed those two together,

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.480
you're gonna end up with another red flower.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.360
But then all of a sudden,

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.080
you get confused when the flower turns out to be pink.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.680
The long and short of what's happening here

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.840
is that the dominant gene is being diluted

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.200
by the recessive gene.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.280
This is what happens with hair color.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.280
See, the actual genes being passed on here

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.960
control how much eumelanin

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.800
and pheomelanin you have in your hair.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.200
And those determine what color your hair is.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.840
Melanin is the natural pigment that's found in organisms.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.520
Eumelanin is a darker pigment,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.200
and pheomelanin is a lighter pigment

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.480
that often skews red.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.960
Typically, eumelanin overpowers pheomelanin.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.640
So if you have a lot of eumelanin,

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.080
then you're gonna have black hair.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.800
If you have almost no eumelanin and more pheomelanin,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.160
then your hair is gonna be red.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:01.680
Blonde and brown hair, then,

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.840
fall into the spectrum between those two colors.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.120
Thanks to incomplete dominance,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.040
your hair is likely gonna be a mix of the colors

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.080
that your parents have.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.040
And this is exactly what we're seeing with Cocomelon.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.120
When you really look at each of the characters,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.040
they all have slightly different hair colors,

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.200
because they have different amounts of eumelanin.

